    "id": "news-1791051994138-0",
    "title": "Report: US Air Operations in Somalia Linked to at Least 216 Civilian Casualties Since 2007",
    "original_title": "US air operations killed or wounded at least 216 civilians in Somalia",
    "slug": "report-us-air-operations-in-somalia-linked-to-at-least-216-civilian-casualties-since-2007",
    "summary": "An investigation by Arab Reporters for Investigative Journalism finds that US air operations in Somalia killed or injured at least 216 civilians between 2007 and May 2025, though the true figure may be higher.",
    "content": "An investigation supported by Arab Reporters for Investigative Journalism (ARIJ) has documented at least 216 civilian casualties—those killed or injured—from US air operations in Somalia between 2007 and May 2025. The figure represents the minimum number of civilians that researchers could verify from available incident reports, indicating that the actual toll may be higher. The investigation does not claim that the United States has confirmed each casualty.\n\nThe ARIJ-backed probe examined US air operations in four countries: Somalia, Syria, Iraq, and Yemen. Among these, Syria recorded the highest number of civilian casualties, with 1,336 documented. While Somalia's toll is lower, the report highlights that US military actions in the country have had a significant impact on civilians over nearly two decades.\n\nUS air operations in Somalia have primarily targeted the militant group al-Shabaab, which has been fighting the Somali government and African Union peacekeepers since 2007. The strikes are part of a broader US counterterrorism strategy in the Horn of Africa. Since 2007, the US has conducted numerous airstrikes and drone attacks, often in support of Somali government forces and international allies. The investigation's findings underscore persistent concerns about the accuracy of targeting and the protection of non-combatants in these operations.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, these findings are deeply troubling. Many have family members in the affected regions and have long called for greater transparency and accountability regarding civilian harm. The diaspora also plays a vital role in supporting relatives back home, and news of civilian casualties can heighten anxiety and distrust. Moreover, the report's release may intensify advocacy efforts by diaspora groups seeking independent investigations and compensation for victims.\n\nThe investigation acknowledges limitations, including potential underreporting due to inaccessible areas and the difficulty of verifying casualties in a conflict zone. The actual number of civilians killed or injured could be higher than the documented 216. The US military has previously stated that it takes measures to avoid civilian casualties but has also acknowledged some incidents.\n\nAs the international community continues to monitor the situation, this report adds to the body of evidence on the human cost of counterterrorism operations. It remains unclear whether the findings will prompt a policy review or changes in operational protocols. For now, the documented civilian toll stands as a reminder of the complex and often devastating consequences of war.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/us-airstrikes-somalia-216-civilians-killed-wounded/",
    "source": "somaliguardian.com",
    "source_name": "Somalia Guardian",
    "original_url": "https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/us-airstrikes-somalia-216-civilians-killed-wounded/",
    "country": "Somalia",
    "category": "Security",
    "published_at": "2026-10-03T17:21:34.000Z",
    "first_seen_at": "2026-10-03T18:26:34.138Z",
    "update_batch": "afternoon",
    "image_url": "https://www.somaliguardian.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/3-7.jpg",
