    "slug": "pro-government-militia-claims-control-of-mekelle-airport-in-ethiopia-s-tigray",
    "summary": "A militia allied with Ethiopia's federal government says it has recaptured Mekelle's Alula Aba Nega Airport, though the claim remains unverified. The move marks a significant development in the ongoing conflict in Tigray.",
    "content": "A militia group aligned with Ethiopia's federal government announced on Friday that it had seized control of the main airport in Mekelle, the capital of the northern Tigray region. The Tigray Peace Forces, a pro-government militia, stated on social media that it had taken full control of Alula Aba Nega Airport and was clearing the city. The claim could not be independently verified, and the Prime Minister's Office did not immediately respond to requests for comment.\n\nThe conflict in Tigray began in November 2020 when Prime Minister Abiy Ahmed ordered a military offensive against the Tigray People's Liberation Front (TPLF), the regional governing party. The TPLF had dominated Ethiopian politics for nearly three decades before Abiy took office in 2018. The war has since involved Eritrean forces and various militias, leading to widespread displacement and human rights abuses.\n\nMekelle, with a population of around half a million, has been a focal point of the conflict. It was briefly captured by Tigrayan forces in June 2021 before federal forces retook it later that year. The airport's strategic importance lies in its capacity to facilitate troop movements and humanitarian aid deliveries. Control of the airport could affect the flow of supplies to a region facing severe shortages.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the situation in Ethiopia is closely watched due to the large Somali population in the country, particularly in the Ogaden region, and the broader Horn of Africa's stability. Many Somali diaspora members have family and business ties across Ethiopia and are concerned about the conflict's spillover effects, including refugee flows and economic disruptions. The diaspora has also been actively involved in humanitarian efforts, raising funds and awareness for those affected by the Tigray crisis.\n\nThe international community has repeatedly called for a ceasefire and unimpeded humanitarian access to Tigray. The United Nations has warned of a humanitarian catastrophe, with millions in need of assistance. The latest development at the airport could either escalate tensions or serve as a precursor to negotiations, depending on the veracity of the claim and the response from Tigrayan forces.\n\nAs the situation evolves, the Somali diaspora remains attentive to the implications for regional stability and the potential for increased displacement. Many are urging peaceful dialogue to resolve the conflict and prevent further suffering.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/africa/pro-government-forces-claim-retake-mekelle-airport-ethiopia-tigray/",
